text:
November 16, 2026

TRENTON – The New Jersey Department of Labor and Workforce Development (NJDOL) Office of Apprenticeship has announced $1.5 million in available grant funding through the New Jersey Builders’ Utilization Initiative for Labor Diversity (NJBUILD) Women and Minority Veterans in Construction Trades (WMVIC) program.

This grant program provides women and minority veterans, including members of the National Guard and Reserve, retirees, and service members transitioning out of active duty, with training to prepare them for Registered Apprenticeship programs or full-time, long-term careers in building and construction. The program aims to remove financial barriers to job training and to connect veterans with quality jobs in the construction industry.

“New Jersey’s veterans deserve a clear path to good career opportunities,” said Acting Labor Commissioner Kevin D. Jarvis. “NJBUILD connects women and minority veterans with hands-on training and real job opportunities in the building trades, strengthening our construction workforce and ensuring that the people building New Jersey reflect the communities they serve.”

Organizations that are awarded grants will work closely with employers and Registered Apprenticeship sponsors to make sure training matches industry needs. They will also provide support services to help veterans secure and succeed in their roles.

“Our veterans bring discipline, teamwork, and a strong work ethic to everything they do,” said Jessica Richardson, NJDOL’s Chief of NJBUILD and Special Initiatives. “NJBUILD gives them the technical training and credentials to put those strengths to work.”

What NJBUILD Offers

Each program participant is expected to receive:

At least 30 hours of job readiness training, such as workplace and employability skills;

At least 120 hours of hands-on, trade-specific construction training;

At least 15 hours of job shadowing;

At least one industry-recognized credential; and

Help finding a job, with the goal of placement in a U.S. Department of Labor Registered Apprenticeship or a full-time construction-related job that pays $17 per hour or more

Qualifying job placements include building and construction trades opportunities (including clean energy and lead abatement), administrative, support, or operational jobs within the construction industry, and Registered Apprenticeship programs in construction.

Since Fiscal Year 2019, NJDOL has awarded more than $24 million in NJBUILD funding through its Women and Minorities in Construction and Women and Minority Veterans in Construction grant programs, supporting the training of more than 2,500 women and minorities in the building and construction trades.

“The NJBUILD program recruits and provides skilled training for women and minority veterans who are in search of a rewarding career in the building trades,” said Bill Mullen, President of the New Jersey Building and Construction Trades Council, a prior NJBUILD grantee. “They sacrifice their lives for us, and we owe them more than a handshake upon returning home.”

Who Can Apply

The following organizations are eligible to apply as the lead applicant:

Any employer based in New Jersey or with a New Jersey presence;

Workforce Intermediary Sponsors;

Employer/Trade Associations;

Any labor organization that represents workers in the occupation for which the apprentices will be trained;

Labor/Management Partnership (public or private);

New Jersey Local Education Agency (LEA);

Public vocational schools within New Jersey;

Public high schools within New Jersey;

Two- and four-year institutions of higher education;

Public or private non-profit organizations (including Community Based Organizations);

Business or trade organizations representing a particular trade, group of trades, contractors, or employers;

Community-based organizations;

New Jersey local Workforce Development Boards;

Economic development organizations;

State, Local, and County governments within New Jersey;

Faith-based organizations; and

Any other private educational or training entity that is on the Eligible Training Provider List (ETPL).

Key dates:

November 23, 1 p.m.: Virtual technical assistance (TA) workshop (pre-registration required at least one business day prior). Register here.

December 2: Letter of Intent due by noon

December 9: Application due by noon

